== 1
{scope}

=== a
{scope}

==== i
{scope}

===== Solution

Extend the given functions continuously to zero by $w_1(0)=w_2(0)=0$. This is forced by their stated differentiability. Their derivatives at zero are both zero, since the corresponding difference quotients tend to zero.

For $x<0$, $w_2=w_2'=0$, and for $x>0$, $w_1=w_1'=0$. At zero all four quantities vanish. Thus the <Wronskian> is
$$
\boxed{W(w_1,w_2)(x)=w_1w_2'-w_1'w_2=0\quad\text{for all }x.}
$$
Nevertheless, if the constant <linear combination> $Aw_1+Bw_2$ vanishes identically, evaluation at $x=-1$ gives $-A=0$, and evaluation at $x=1$ gives $B=0$. Therefore **the two functions are <linearly independent>**.

This illustrates why a <vanishing Wronskian does not imply linear dependence> for arbitrary <differentiable functions>. Here both functions are continuously differentiable, but neither has a second derivative at zero. The common regular differential equation hypothesis in the next part is absent.

==== ii
{scope}

===== Solution

Differentiate the <Wronskian> using the <product rule> and substitute the common <homogeneous linear differential equation>:
$$
\begin{aligned}
W'&=(y_1y_2'-y_1'y_2)'=y_1y_2''-y_1''y_2\\
&=y_1(-py_2'-qy_2)-(-py_1'-qy_1)y_2=-pW.
\end{aligned}
$$
Multiplying this first-order equation by the <integrating factor> $\exp(\int_{x_0}^x p(t)\,dt)$ and integrating gives <Abel's identity>:
$$
\boxed{W(x)=W(x_0)\exp\!\left(-\int_{x_0}^x p(t)\,dt\right).}
$$
The exponential factor is finite and nonzero for every $x$ in the given <interval>, because $p$ is continuous on the compact interval between $x_0$ and $x$. Hence $W(x_0)\ne0$ implies $W(x)\ne0$ everywhere in that interval. Using an integrating factor avoids dividing by $W$, which would assume the conclusion.

=== b
{scope}

==== i
{scope}

===== Solution

Interpret the <Dirac delta> coefficient as a <point interaction in a second-order differential equation>. The solution is continuous at $x=1$ and is twice differentiable on either side, where $y''=f$. Its <transmission conditions for a delta coefficient> are
$$
[y]_1=0,\qquad [y']_1=-\alpha y(1).
$$
Indeed, the <distributional second derivative across a jump> contributes $[y']_1\delta(x-1)$, which must cancel the delta coefficient because $f$ has no point source.

Put $s=y'(0)$ and $F(x)=\int_0^x(x-t)f(t)\,dt$. Integrating the ordinary equation and inserting the required derivative jump gives
$$
y(x)=c_1+sx+F(x)-\alpha y(1)(x-1)_+,\qquad y(1)=c_1+s+F(1),
$$
where $(x-1)_+=\max(x-1,0)$. This formula is continuous, has the stated jump, satisfies the equation, and parametrizes every solution with the left endpoint value. Imposing the right endpoint value gives the scalar equation
$$
\bigl[\pi-\alpha(\pi-1)\bigr]s
=c_2-c_1-F(\pi)+\alpha(\pi-1)\bigl(c_1+F(1)\bigr).
$$
Consequently a unique $s$, and hence a unique solution for every $f,c_1,c_2$, exists precisely when
$$
\boxed{\alpha\ne\alpha_*:=\frac\pi{\pi-1}.}
$$
At the exceptional value, the nonzero homogeneous solution with zero endpoint values is
$$
\phi(x)=\begin{cases}x,&0\leq x\leq1,\\(\pi-x)/(\pi-1),&1\leq x\leq\pi.\end{cases}
$$
It has derivative jump $-\pi/(\pi-1)=-\alpha_*\phi(1)$. Thus uniqueness fails. More precisely, there are infinitely many solutions if
$$
\int_0^\pi\phi(x)f(x)\,dx=c_1+\frac{c_2}{\pi-1},
$$
and none otherwise. This follows either from the scalar equation or by <integration by parts> against $\phi$. It is the compatibility obstruction at <Dirichlet resonance for a point interaction>.

==== ii
{scope}

===== Solution

Use a <Green function> satisfying $L_xG(x,\xi)=\delta(x-\xi)$ with homogeneous <Dirichlet boundary conditions> $G(0,\xi)=G(\pi,\xi)=0$, for $0<\xi<\pi$. Since $G_{xx}=0$ away from $x=1$ and $x=\xi$, it is continuous and piecewise affine, with possible derivative jumps at those two locations.

For $\xi\ne1$, the <transmission conditions for a delta coefficient> and the unit source condition are
$$
\begin{aligned}
[G]_1&=0,&[G_x]_1&=-G(1,\xi),\\
[G]_\xi&=0,&[G_x]_\xi&=1.
\end{aligned}
$$
If $\xi=1$, there is a single junction, and the correct combined condition is
$$
\boxed{[G]_1=0,\qquad [G_x]_1+G(1,1)=1.}
$$
For example, a suitable piecewise form when $0<\xi<1$ is
$$
G(x,\xi)=\begin{cases}A x,&0\leq x\leq\xi,\\B x+C,&\xi\leq x\leq1,\\D(\pi-x),&1\leq x\leq\pi,\end{cases}
$$
where the coefficients depend on $\xi$. The two continuity conditions, $B-A=1$ and $-D-B=-G(1,\xi)$ determine them. When $1<\xi<\pi$, use the same affine form on the intervals cut at $1,\xi$, with $B-A=-G(1,\xi)$ and $-D-B=1$ instead. This gives all requested boundary and jump conditions without needing the coefficients.

An explicit compact kernel also makes the construction transparent. The left and right homogeneous solutions at $\alpha=1$ are
$$
u(x)=\begin{cases}x,&x\leq1,\\1,&x\geq1,\end{cases}
\qquad
v(x)=\begin{cases}1+(\pi-2)x,&x\leq1,\\\pi-x,&x\geq1.\end{cases}
$$
They obey the interaction jump and have <Wronskian> $uv'-u'v=-1$ on either side. Therefore
$$
\boxed{G(x,\xi)=-u\bigl(\min(x,\xi)\bigr)v\bigl(\max(x,\xi)\bigr).}
$$
Differentiating its two branches at the source gives a unit derivative jump whenever $\xi\ne1$. At $\xi=1$, $G(1,1)=-(\pi-1)$ and $[G_x]_1=\pi$, so the combined condition is also satisfied. This is the <Dirichlet Green function for a point interaction> specialized to the present parameters.

The Green kernel itself has zero endpoint values. The prescribed nonzero endpoint values enter through the homogeneous lift:
$$
\boxed{y(x)=c_1v(x)+c_2u(x)+\int_0^\pi G(x,\xi)f(\xi)\,d\xi.}
$$
Indeed, $v(0)=u(\pi)=1$ and $u(0)=v(\pi)=0$. The kernel accounts for the forcing; the lift accounts for the boundary data. Uniqueness follows from part (i), since $1\ne\pi/(\pi-1)$.

\Image[2017-point-interaction-green-function.png]
{title=The resonant homogeneous mode and the derivative jumps of the Green kernel}
{description=Original calculations for an interaction at $x=1$ on $(0,\pi)$. The left panel varies the coefficient with a fixed initial slope; the resonant curve alone meets the zero right boundary. The right panel shows three source positions at coefficient one, including a source coinciding with the interaction.}

== 2
{scope}

=== a
{scope}

==== i
{scope}

===== Solution

Use <integration by parts> with a <complex conjugate>, so that the argument also covers complex-valued <eigenfunctions>. The coefficient functions are understood to be real. For a nonzero <eigenfunction> $u$ with <Dirichlet boundary conditions>,
$$
\begin{aligned}
\lambda\int_\alpha^\beta |u|^2\,dx
&=\int_\alpha^\beta\overline u\,Mu\,dx\\
&=\bigl[a\overline u\,u'\bigr]_\alpha^\beta-\int_\alpha^\beta a|u'|^2\,dx+\int_\alpha^\beta b|u|^2\,dx\\
&=-\int_\alpha^\beta a|u'|^2\,dx+\int_\alpha^\beta b|u|^2\,dx.
\end{aligned}
$$
The denominator is strictly positive and the numerator is real. Consequently the <eigenvalue> satisfies
$$
\boxed{\lambda=\frac{\int_\alpha^\beta(b|u|^2-a|u'|^2)\,dx}{\int_\alpha^\beta|u|^2\,dx}\in\mathbb R.}
$$
This is the <Rayleigh quotient> for the sign convention used here. Positivity of $a$ is unnecessary for this particular reality argument; real coefficients and disappearance of the endpoint term are what matter.

==== ii
{scope}

===== Solution

The <Lagrange identity for a Sturm-Liouville operator> gives
$$
\int_\alpha^\beta\bigl(\overline u\,Mv-\overline{Mu}\,v\bigr)\,dx
=\bigl[a(\overline u\,v'-\overline{u'}\,v)\bigr]_\alpha^\beta.
$$
In the intended class of <functions> whose values and first <derivatives> stay finite at the endpoints, the endpoint factor $a$ makes this entire boundary term vanish. Thus **no separately prescribed endpoint values are needed to eliminate the boundary form**. The same <integration by parts> argument then proves that the <eigenvalues> are real.

The admissible endpoint behaviour is part of this conclusion: it is the <vanishing endpoint coefficient and the Sturm-Liouville boundary form>, together with endpoint regularity, that replaces explicit <boundary conditions>. If singular <functions> are allowed, a vanishing coefficient alone need not suffice: $a(x)=x(1-x)$ and $v(x)=\log x$ have $a(x)v'(x)\to1$ as $x\downarrow0$. A <self-adjoint differential operator> therefore still needs a specified domain of admissible <functions>.

=== b
{scope}

==== i
{scope}

===== Solution

The useful <change of independent variable in a second-order ODE> is $t=\log x$, followed by $y(x)=xv(t)$. The <chain rule> gives
$$
y'=v+v_t,\qquad y''=x^{-1}(v_t+v_{tt}),\qquad Ly=xv_{tt}.
$$
Thus the <eigenvalue> equation becomes $v_{tt}=\lambda v$ on $0<t<1$, and the endpoint values become $v(0)=v(1)=0$. At the specified <eigenvalues>,
$$
v(t)=A\cos(k\pi t)+B\sin(k\pi t).
$$
The first endpoint forces $A=0$; the second holds for every positive integer $k$. Choosing a convenient normalization yields
$$
\boxed{y_k(x)=x\sin(k\pi\log x),\qquad k=1,2,\ldots.}
$$
Each nonzero constant multiple is the same <eigenfunction> up to normalization. This <logarithmic conjugacy of a repeated-root Euler operator> turns the <Cauchy-Euler equation> into the familiar <Fourier sine series> problem on a unit <interval>.

==== ii
{scope}

===== Solution

Match the coefficient of $y'$ with the <derivative> of the coefficient of $y''$. For <Sturm-Liouville form> this requires
$$
(rx^2)'=-rx,\qquad x^2r'+3xr=0.
$$
A positive <integrating factor> is therefore $r(x)=x^{-3}$, with any positive constant multiple equally valid. Direct calculation gives
$$
\boxed{r=x^{-3},\qquad\widehat Ly=(x^{-1}y')'+x^{-3}y.}
$$
This uses the plus-divergence sign convention of the question. The usual minus-divergence <Sturm-Liouville operator> is its negative; no extra sign is inserted into the requested <eigenvalue> equation.

Because $r$ never vanishes on the <interval>, multiplying the original <eigenvalue> equation by $r$ leaves the requested weighted <eigenvalue> problem unchanged:
$$
\boxed{\widehat\lambda_k=-k^2\pi^2,\qquad\widehat y_k=x\sin(k\pi\log x).}
$$
The weight in the <inner product> is essential. The substitution $t=\log x$ gives
$$
\int_1^e r\widehat y_j\widehat y_k\,dx
=\int_0^1\sin(j\pi t)\sin(k\pi t)\,dt
=\begin{cases}\tfrac12,&j=k,\\0,&j\ne k.\end{cases}
$$
Thus <orthogonality of Sturm-Liouville eigenfunctions> holds for the <weighted inner product> with weight $x^{-3}$, and the squared <norm> is $N_k=1/2$.

==== iii
{scope}

===== Solution

Use the normalization from part (ii), and write
$$
N_k=\int_1^e r\widehat y_k^2\,dx=\frac12,\qquad
I_k=\int_1^e r\widehat y_k e^x\,dx
=\int_1^e\frac{e^x}{x^2}\sin(k\pi\log x)\,dx.
$$
The <Sturm-Liouville eigenfunction expansion> coefficient is $c_k=N_k^{-1}\int_1^e ry\widehat y_k\,dx$. Although $\widehat y_k$ has zero endpoint values, $y$ generally does not. Retain the endpoint term in the <Lagrange identity for a Sturm-Liouville operator>:
$$
\begin{aligned}
\int_1^e\bigl(\widehat y_k\widehat Ly-y\widehat L\widehat y_k\bigr)\,dx
&=\bigl[x^{-1}(\widehat y_k y'-\widehat y_k' y)\bigr]_1^e\\
&=\alpha\widehat y_k'(1)=\alpha k\pi.
\end{aligned}
$$
The positive sign comes from subtracting the contribution at $x=1$. The differential equation also gives $\widehat Ly=re^x-(\beta-1)ry$. Substitution into the preceding identity yields
$$
I_k-(\beta-1+\widehat\lambda_k)c_kN_k=\alpha k\pi.
$$
Hence, away from <resonance in a differential equation>,
$$
\boxed{c_k=\frac{2(I_k-\alpha k\pi)}{\beta-1-k^2\pi^2}
=\frac{2\left[\alpha k\pi-\int_1^e e^x x^{-2}\sin(k\pi\log x)\,dx\right]}{1+k^2\pi^2-\beta}.}
$$
This is the effect of <boundary data in a Sturm-Liouville eigenfunction expansion>: omitting the endpoint contribution would discard the dependence on $\alpha$.

If $\beta=1+n^2\pi^2$ for a positive integer $n$, the <Fredholm solvability condition> is
$$
\boxed{I_n=\alpha n\pi.}
$$
When it fails, there is **no solution**. When it holds, $c_n$ is arbitrary and the other coefficients retain the displayed formula, giving **infinitely many solutions** differing by a multiple of $\widehat y_n$. Sufficiency follows by subtracting the smooth lift $h(x)=\alpha x(1-\log x)$, which has the required endpoint values and satisfies $Lh=0$, and applying the <Fredholm alternative> to the resulting homogeneous <Dirichlet boundary conditions>.

Finally, for $\alpha\ne0$, the displayed <Sturm-Liouville eigenfunction expansion> represents the solution on $1<x<e$ and in the <weighted inner product> sense. Every term is zero at $x=1$, so that series cannot give the nonzero prescribed value by substitution at the endpoint. The <boundary trace> must be imposed separately. Equivalently, expand $y-h$ with homogeneous <Dirichlet boundary conditions> and add $h$ back afterwards.

== 3
{scope}

=== a
{scope}

==== i
{scope}

===== Solution

Move the expansion point to the origin with $z=x+1$. The transformed <ordinary differential equation> is
$$
zy_{zz}+(z-1)y_z+cy=0.
$$
After division by $z$, the functions $z(1-1/z)=z-1$ and $z^2(c/z)=cz$ are <analytic functions> at zero, so this is a <regular singular point>. For a <Frobenius solution> with leading term $a_0z^\rho$, $a_0\ne0$, the lowest power is $z^{\rho-1}$ and its coefficient is
$$
a_0\bigl[\rho(\rho-1)-\rho\bigr]=a_0\rho(\rho-2).
$$
Therefore the <indicial equation> and its <indicial exponents> are
$$
\boxed{\rho(\rho-2)=0,\qquad\rho=0,\ 2.}
$$
The exponent $\rho$ denotes the quantity called $\alpha$ in the paper, and avoids confusion with other uses of that letter.

==== ii
{scope}

===== Solution

Substitute a <Frobenius series> $y=\sum_{n\geq0}a_nz^{n+\rho}$ into the transformed equation. For $n\geq1$, equating the coefficient of $z^{n+\rho-1}$ gives the <recurrence relation>
$$
(n+\rho)(n+\rho-2)a_n+(n+\rho-1+c)a_{n-1}=0.
$$
At the larger <indicial root> $\rho=2$, the coefficient of $a_n$ is $n(n+2)\ne0$, so a <Frobenius solution> always exists. At the smaller <indicial root> $\rho=0$, the first two equations are
$$
a_1=ca_0,\qquad0\cdot a_2+(1+c)a_1=0.
$$
Since $a_0\ne0$, the exceptional equation is consistent exactly when
$$
\boxed{c(c+1)=0,\qquad c=0\ \text{or}\ c=-1.}
$$
These are **both admissible values**, including the zero value; the singular wording of the question does not remove either. This is the <undetermined coefficient at Frobenius resonance>: at either value $a_2$ is free, whereas otherwise the second independent solution requires a <logarithmic Frobenius solution>.

Explicit pairs demonstrate sufficiency and <linear independence>. In the variable $z=x+1$ one may take
$$
\begin{array}{c|cc}
c&u_0(z)&u_2(z)\\\hline
0&1&1-(1+z)e^{-z}\\
-1&1-z&e^{-z}+z-1.
\end{array}
$$
Both displayed $u_2$ have leading term $z^2/2$, while $u_0(0)=1$. They therefore give independent <Frobenius solutions> with the two <indicial exponents>, and direct <differentiation> verifies the equation. The point is an <apparent regular singular point> in these two cases.

For the requested closed forms, particularly short choices are
$$
\boxed{y(x)=1\quad(c=0),\qquad y(x)=x\quad(c=-1).}
$$
In addition, $(x+2)e^{-x}$ solves the zero-parameter equation and $e^{-x}$ solves the equation with $c=-1$.

=== b
{scope}

==== i
{scope}

===== Solution

The leading <regular perturbation> equation has $c=0$. Its homogeneous solutions are $1$ and $(x+2)e^{-x}$, so
$$
y_0(x)=A+B(x+2)e^{-x}.
$$
The boundary data must also be expanded: $2e^\epsilon=2+2\epsilon+\cdots$, while the right endpoint value is independent of $\epsilon$. Thus $y_0(0)=2$ and $y_0(1)=3/e$, giving
$$
A+2B=2,\qquad A+\frac3eB=\frac3e.
$$
Since $2-3/e\ne0$, the unique values are $A=0$, $B=1$. Therefore
$$
\boxed{y_0(x)=(x+2)e^{-x}.}
$$
Its <derivatives> are $y_0'=-(x+1)e^{-x}$ and $y_0''=xe^{-x}$, so the leading differential equation and both leading endpoint values can be checked directly.

\Image[2017-logarithmic-modes-and-perturbation.png]
{title=Logarithmic eigenmodes and the leading regular perturbation solution}
{description=The left panel shows three eigenfunctions after division by x and the change of variable t = log x. The right panel compares the derived leading term with numerical boundary value solutions at two small parameter values; these are original calculations.}

==== ii
{scope}

===== Solution

Let $L_0u=(x+1)u''+xu'$. Matching powers in the <regular perturbation> expansion and in the <Taylor series> of the left endpoint value gives the complete recursive <boundary value problem>
$$
\boxed{L_0y_n=-y_{n-1},\qquad y_n(0)=\frac2{n!},\quad y_n(1)=0,\qquad n\geq1.}
$$
The higher-order left endpoint values are not zero. The <parameter-dependent boundary data in a regular perturbation hierarchy> rule applies here.

First prove uniqueness for any one of these forced problems. The difference $v$ of two solutions obeys $L_0v=0$ and homogeneous <Dirichlet boundary conditions>. The positive <integrating factor> $r_0=e^x/(x+1)^2$ gives
$$
r_0L_0v=(pv')',\qquad p=\frac{e^x}{x+1}>0.
$$
Multiplication by $\overline v$ and <integration by parts> yield
$$
0=\int_0^1\overline v\,(pv')'\,dx=-\int_0^1p|v'|^2\,dx.
$$
Hence $v'=0$, and the endpoint values force $v=0$. The <kernel> is trivial: zero is not an <eigenvalue> of this homogeneous <Dirichlet boundary value problem>.

Existence is also explicit. For arbitrary smooth forcing $g$ and endpoint values $u(0)=a$, $u(1)=b$, two integrations of $(pu')'=r_0g$ give
$$
u(x)=a+C H(x)+J_g(x),
$$
where
$$
H(x)=\int_0^x(s+1)e^{-s}\,ds=2-(x+2)e^{-x},
\qquad
J_g(x)=\int_0^x(s+1)e^{-s}\int_0^s\frac{e^tg(t)}{(t+1)^2}\,dt\,ds.
$$
Since $H(1)=2-3/e>0$, the unique choice
$$
\boxed{C=\frac{b-a-J_g(1)}{2-3/e}}
$$
always meets the second endpoint value. Starting from the smooth $y_0$, induction with $g=-y_{n-1}$, $a=2/n!$, $b=0$ proves **existence and uniqueness at every order**.

Equivalently, the <Fredholm alternative> has no compatibility obstruction because the homogeneous <kernel> vanishes; the corresponding <adjoint operator> has no zero <eigenvalue> either. The direct construction above proves this without computing any particular $y_n$ beyond the leading term.
